@@ -98,6 +98,57 @@ is then actually required to exist. Nothing in this guidance enables gating; the
 check-less warning path preserves the required-deliverable contract so it stays
 enforceable when gating lands.
 
+## Cargo (Rust) acceptance checks
+
+A Rust stage can prove its criteria with a cargo command, but cargo is
+approval-only by default. The operator opts in per project:
+
+```yaml
+validation:
+  acceptance_checks:
+    cargo: true
+```
+
+With the switch on, a cargo check is accepted only under a **default-deny
+argument policy** — the subcommand and every flag must match an allow-list, so an
+unknown or future flag is refused:
+
+| Command | Allowed arguments (each at most once) |
+| ------- | ------------------------------------- |
+| `cargo build` / `cargo test` / `cargo clippy` | `--locked`, `--all-targets`, `--manifest-path PATH` |
+| `cargo clippy` | the above, optionally ending in the exact suffix `-- -D warnings` |
+| `cargo fmt` | `--manifest-path PATH`, `--all`, and a **required** `--check` |
+
+Everything else stays `REQUIRES_APPROVAL`: `cargo run`/`install`/`publish`/`add`/
+`remove`/`fix`, `--target` (which also names a custom target-spec path),
+`--target-dir`, `--artifact-dir`, `--config`, `-Z`, unknown flags, a `+toolchain`
+override, positional arguments, a repeated flag, and any argument after `--` other
+than clippy's exact `-- -D warnings` suffix. Because cargo executes project code
+(build scripts, proc-macros, the test harness), enabling this is a trust
+escalation. A project `Approve`/`Deny` prefix, when one is supplied, can only
+tighten further; no project configuration currently supplies one, so the
+default-deny allow-list above is the restriction in force today.
+
+Commands run without a shell, so `cargo test` runs in the repository root; for a
+package in a subdirectory use `--manifest-path`:
+
+```markdown
+- **Acceptance Checks:** `cargo test --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml`
+- **Acceptance Checks:** `cargo fmt --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --check`
+```
+
+`--manifest-path` (both `--manifest-path VALUE` and `--manifest-path=VALUE`) is
+**confined to the repository root**: an empty value, an absolute path, a `..`
+component, or a path whose nearest existing ancestor symlink-resolves outside the
+root is refused before the check runs.
+
+**Containment is not a sandbox.** It only limits *which manifest file* a check may
+name. A confined crate still runs its build scripts and proc-macros
+(`cargo build`/`clippy`), still runs its test harness (`cargo test`), and still
+fetches its dependencies from crates.io — none of that is confined by the
+manifest-path check. Treat an enabled cargo acceptance check as arbitrary project
+code.
+
